𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄𝗲𝗿:
How will you upgrade an EKS cluster?
Have you done any upgrades before?
𝗖𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗲:
Yes, I’ve worked on EKS cluster upgrades.
Upgrading an EKS cluster is not just clicking upgrade version, it’s a structured process with several prerequisites. I always follow a proper checklist before starting.
𝙄𝙣𝙩𝙚𝙧𝙫𝙞𝙚𝙬𝙚𝙧:
Ok, then what are the prerequisites?
𝘾𝙖𝙣𝙙𝙞𝙙𝙖𝙩𝙚:
Before upgrading, I make sure of these key checks:
1. 𝘾𝙤𝙧𝙙𝙤𝙣 𝙣𝙤𝙙𝙚𝙨 – To prevent new pods from scheduling on nodes that will be upgraded.
2. 𝙍𝙚𝙖𝙙 𝙩𝙝𝙚 𝙧𝙚𝙡𝙚𝙖𝙨𝙚 𝙣𝙤𝙩𝙚𝙨 – Understand what changed between versions (ex: v1.30 → v1.31).
3. 𝙃𝙖𝙫𝙚 𝙖 𝙧𝙤𝙡𝙡𝙗𝙖𝙘𝙠 𝙨𝙩𝙧𝙖𝙩𝙚𝙜𝙮 – Since Kubernetes does NOT support downgrades, I ensure backups and lower environments are tested.
4. 𝙑𝙖𝙡𝙞𝙙𝙖𝙩𝙚 𝙞𝙣 𝘿𝙚𝙫/𝙌𝘼 𝙛𝙞𝙧𝙨𝙩 – Never touch production directly.
5. 𝘾𝙝𝙚𝙘𝙠 𝙫𝙚𝙧𝙨𝙞𝙤𝙣 𝙘𝙤𝙢𝙥𝙖𝙩𝙞𝙗𝙞𝙡𝙞𝙩𝙮 – Control plane, nodes, kubelet, and add-ons must align.
6. Ensure Cluster Autoscaler is compatible with the target version.
7. 𝙑𝙚𝙧𝙞𝙛𝙮 𝘼𝙙𝙙-𝙤𝙣 𝙘𝙤𝙢𝙥𝙖𝙩𝙞𝙗𝙞𝙡𝙞𝙩𝙮 – VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, and all custom add-ons.
8. 𝘾𝙝𝙚𝙘𝙠 𝙠𝙪𝙗𝙚𝙡𝙚𝙩 𝙫𝙚𝙧𝙨𝙞𝙤𝙣𝙨 – Nodes cannot be ahead of the control plane.

𝙄𝙣𝙩𝙚𝙧𝙫𝙞𝙚𝙬𝙚𝙧:
Good. After the prerequisites, how do you actually perform the upgrade?
𝘾𝙖𝙣𝙙𝙞𝙙𝙖𝙩𝙚:
Sure, it’s a 3-step formula:
1. 𝙐𝙥𝙜𝙧𝙖𝙙𝙚 𝘾𝙤𝙣𝙩𝙧𝙤𝙡 𝙋𝙡𝙖𝙣𝙚
AWS handles it. I trigger it using eksctl or console.
2. 𝙐𝙥𝙜𝙧𝙖𝙙𝙚 𝙉𝙤𝙙𝙚 𝙂𝙧𝙤𝙪𝙥𝙨
Managed nodes → in-place upgrade.
Self-managed → create new group → cordon & drain → migrate pods.
3. 𝙐𝙥𝙜𝙧𝙖𝙙𝙚 𝘼𝙡𝙡 𝘼𝙙𝙙-𝙤𝙣𝙨
Because networking & DNS must match the new version.
--> VPC CNI
--> CoreDNS
--> Kube-proxy
--> ALB controller
--> Observability/logging tools

 𝙄𝙣𝙩𝙚𝙧𝙫𝙞𝙚𝙬𝙚𝙧:
Why can't we downgrade the cluster if something goes wrong?
𝘾𝙖𝙣𝙙𝙞𝙙𝙖𝙩𝙚:
Because Kubernetes doesn’t support downgrading.
Upgrades remove old API versions, change etcd schema, and modify controller behavior.
𝗔 𝗱𝗼𝘄𝗻𝗴𝗿𝗮𝗱𝗲 𝘄𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗯𝗿𝗲𝗮𝗸:
--> API servers
--> CRDs
--> DNS
--> Networking
--> Controllers
So the only safe rollback is:
Create a new cluster on the old version → migrate workloads.

𝙄𝙣𝙩𝙚𝙧𝙫𝙞𝙚𝙬𝙚𝙧:
 Anything else you check post-upgrade?
𝘾𝙖𝙣𝙙𝙞𝙙𝙖𝙩𝙚:
Yes, I perform:
--> Pod health & readiness checks
--> Autoscaling tests
--> Logs & metrics validation
--> Ingress & load balancing checks
--> Version verification for all add-ons

EKS upgrades are ecosystem upgrades, not just Kubernetes version upgrades. Control plane, nodes, networking, DNS, and all add-ons must be upgraded together for the cluster to stay healthy and stable.
